CHAPTERS = [
    ("Electric motors", "A three-phase induction motor produces torque through a rotating magnetic field. The stator creates that field; the rotor turns slightly slower than synchronous speed under load. Read voltage, connection, frequency, current, duty and insulation data from the nameplate. Synchronous speed is 120 × frequency / poles. Mechanical output power and electrical input power differ by efficiency."),
    ("Protection", "Short-circuit protection and overload protection perform different jobs. A fuse or circuit breaker interrupts fault current; an overload relay responds to sustained motor overload. Phase monitoring can detect supply problems, and embedded temperature sensors monitor winding temperature. Select and coordinate devices from manufacturer data for the actual installation."),
    ("Disconnectors and manual starters", "A disconnector provides isolation when selected and used for that purpose. A manual motor starter combines manual switching with protective functions specified by its manufacturer. A contactor is an operating device and is not automatically an isolator. Identify each device by its markings and ratings."),
    ("Control and signaling", "Normally open contacts conduct when actuated; normally closed contacts open when actuated. A seal-in branch uses a contactor's auxiliary contact to maintain a run command after a start button is released. Place stop and protective permissives in series with the holding logic. Indicator lights report modeled states; they do not prove absence of voltage."),
    ("Panel components", "Control supplies, terminal blocks, auxiliary relays, labels, cable ducts and enclosures support the switching devices. Keep a consistent tag scheme and distinguish power and control circuits. Coil voltage, contact ratings, environment, heat dissipation and access requirements affect component selection."),
    ("Star-delta starting", "A compatible six-terminal motor starts with its windings in star and runs in delta. The starter needs main, star and delta contactors. Star and delta must never close together. An open transition introduces dead time between them. The motor's rated delta voltage must match the supply. This model includes a star timer, transition timer and mutual interlock."),
    ("Autotransformer starting", "An autotransformer supplies reduced voltage during acceleration before transfer to the full supply. Reducing voltage reduces starting current and torque. Tap selection and transition arrangements depend on the motor, driven load and starter design. This differs from a star-delta connection change."),
    ("Series-parallel starting", "Some motors permit winding reconnection to reduce starting voltage. The winding arrangement and terminal diagram determine whether series-parallel starting is supported. Never infer compatibility from terminal count alone. Follow the motor's documented connection scheme."),
    ("Wound-rotor acceleration", "A wound-rotor motor exposes rotor connections through slip rings. External rotor resistance can shape starting current and torque, then be removed in stages. This method requires equipment intended for the rotor circuit and differs from stator voltage reduction."),
    ("Two-speed and Dahlander motors", "A two-speed motor can use separate windings or a pole-changing winding. Dahlander connections change the pole arrangement, usually producing a 2:1 synchronous speed ratio. The exact terminal connections depend on the motor design. Interlock incompatible contactors during speed changes."),
    ("Motor braking", "Coasting, mechanical braking, DC injection and drive-controlled braking produce different stopping behavior. Regenerative braking returns energy where the system supports it; braking resistors dissipate energy as heat. A stop command is not electrical isolation. Select braking equipment for the load inertia and duty."),
    ("Soft starters, VFDs and servo drives", "A soft starter controls applied voltage during starting and stopping. A VFD controls motor frequency and voltage to regulate speed. A servo drive normally closes a feedback loop for position, speed or torque. Drive enable, run command, fault status and speed reference are separate signals in the simulator."),
    ("Control solutions and timers", "An on-delay timer asserts its output after a continuously true input reaches the preset duration. Removing the input resets this non-retentive TON. Off-delay, pulse and flasher timers have different timing diagrams. This engine implements TON explicitly; a scripted traffic sequence demonstrates phase progression."),
    ("PLC logic and scan cycles", "Each scan reads an input snapshot, evaluates all rungs using the previous coil state, checks interlocks, then commits new outputs. Rung order does not make a newly calculated coil visible in the same scan. Invalid scans leave timers and outputs unchanged. Structured Text export keeps the same snapshot and commit structure."),
    ("Safety and NR-10 awareness", "This application is a learning simulator and does not control equipment. Physical electrical work requires appropriate competence, procedures and local requirements. Use an established isolation and verification procedure before work. Software interlocks and simulated readings are not safety-rated protective functions or evidence that an installation is safe.")]

